Z||000000||flexableface||09-18-2000||09:22 AM||||hey i was wondering the effects of cut the stock springs on an 86 se. I dont have a pot of money and i just wanted to know the pro or cons if any at all.  thanks luke||||reg||1
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||09-18-2000||10:07 AM||||If done correctly, the car will look cooler, and have a lower center of gravity. Giving you better handling in the corners. Unfortunatly it also creats 'bad geometry' with problems like rough ride and tire wear.<P>if done incorrectly... horride handling characteristics, tire wear, poor alignment, rough ride, and just all round not good. There is a company the sells drop spindals for 84-87 fieros. check out <A HREF="http://www.iserv.net/%7Estrdream/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.iserv.net/%7Estrdream/index.html</A> <P>they stopped for a while, but they may have started up again.||||reg||1
Z||000002||2xsess||09-18-2000||12:13 PM||||If you go to <A HREF="http://www.carparts.com" TARGET=_blank>www.carparts.com</A>  they have belltech dropped springs for around $150. I think its a 1.5" drop.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||09-18-2000||06:25 PM||||Carparts.com didn't have any dropped spindles or lowering springs when I checked a few minutes ago.  Someone could make tons of money if they offered dropped spindles for '84-'87s at a reasonable price.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ThatRickGuy||09-18-2000||06:29 PM||||that site I threw out there said that they 'had' 84-87 drop spindles, but were sold out and wouldn't start reproducing them for a while. my question tho... When will someone make drop spindles for the '88s!?!?! the only non-chop job I've heard of is the deal with using caddy parts and flipping stuff arround...||||reg||1
Z||000005||RedOktober||09-18-2000||06:37 PM||||My suggestion is NEVER Chop coil springs. From my understandings, coil springs ate tempered steel, and dont take lightly to being cut using a torch. the other reason is that your car will bounce around over the slightest marble on the road and thus rattling your brains out.<BR>If you want a lower ride save your pennies and get a proper set of springs, ones that have the same or similar spring rate, but just wound shorter. You will be happier.<BR>JC Whittney's (spelling???) has some for around $75.00 a pair, You will need 2 sets, 1 for the front and 1 for the rear.<BR>Good luck<BR>  <P>------------------<BR>86 SE V6 Auto Turbo Going 3.4 OHV<BR>86 2M4 5sp (Beater)||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||09-18-2000||07:02 PM||||I say Chop-Chop!! Go for it! I've cut springs on several of my cars without any problems. They handle and ride great, and go through the alignment shop with no red marks. I know of a certain replicar manufacturer that cuts the spings on every car they build (except the ones that get the fancy [$$$] suspension) and they have never had a complaint. I even went as far as heating the top coil with a torch and reforming it to fit the perch on my F-150. I must have put 50,000 miles on that without problems. If you don't like the idea of using a torch on a spring, just use an abrasive blade - chop saw or muffler cut-off tool. The JC Whitney springs are by Suspension Techniques.<P>This ex-fiero is riding on torch-cut coils, if that doesn't convince you to do it, I don't know what will!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/lfclosed.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||14
Z||000007||2xsess||09-18-2000||08:07 PM||||I just checked carparts.com and they have 1.5" springs for 84-87 Fieros, cost $165.Go into performance then lowering kits and there it is.<P><BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
